BT 1 0 0 1 0 111.2 Tm /F1 8.8 Tf 12 TL (Palo Alto Unit 42 \(3 Aug\) demonstrated three post-compromise attacks against Google Password Manager's synced-passkey) Tj T* (workflow in Chrome on Windows \(TPM-backed\). All require malware already running on the host and do NOT break passkey) Tj T* (cryptography. 'Pass-ta-key' plants an attacker-controlled user-verification key; 'Silver Pass-ta-key' abuses device re-enrollment) Tj T* (to register the attacker's own UV key and log in as the victim from the attacker's machine; 'Golden Pass-ta-key' recovers the) Tj T* (security-domain secret that protects synced passkeys - which briefly appears in Chrome's process memory - letting malware) Tj T* (decrypt synchronised passkey records and their private keys. Two paths give reusable access from another machine. Critically,) Tj T* (Google's implementation currently offers no way to rotate/revoke the security-domain secret, so a thief can retain access to) Tj T* (existing and future synced passkeys. This is a strong reminder that passkeys are only as strong as the endpoint: prioritise) Tj T* (endpoint hardening/EDR, hardware security keys for the highest-value accounts, and watch for device re-enrollment events on) Tj T* (Windows.) Tj T* ET
